ion: none; border-bottom: 1.11111px solid rgb(220, 223, 229);\">Cloud &amp; Apps<!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<td class=\"cOeeGf\" colspan=\"undefined\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1.11111px solid rgb(220, 223, 229);\">Provides <span data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><a class=\"H23r4e\" href=\"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-hveid=\"CAAIJhAC\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 500; margin: 0px; text-decoration: underline 1px rgb(21, 88, 214); border-bottom: 0px rgb(21, 88, 214);\">S3-compatible</a><!--TgQPHd|[[&quot;https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/&quot;,null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[{&quot;1218&quot;:[17]}]],17,null,&quot;Ceph Storage Cluster&quot;,&quot;APIs. Most Ceph deployments use Ceph Block Devices, Ceph Object Storage and/or the Ceph File System. You may also develop applications that talk directly to the Ceph Storage Cluster. ... The Ceph Documentation is a community resource funded and hosted by the non-profit Ceph Foundation. If you would like to support this and our other efforts, please consider joining now.&quot;,null,&quot;Ceph Documentation&quot;,&quot;https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/faviconV2?url\\u003dhttps://docs.ceph.com\\u0026client\\u003dAIM\\u0026size\\u003d64\\u0026type\\u003dFAVICON\\u0026enable_upscaled\\u003dtrue\\u0026fallback_opts\\u003dTYPE,SIZE,URL&quot;,[[1778331985474350,107769412,1729808051],null,null,null,null,[[0,34,127]]]]]--></span> and Swift APIs for unstructured data.<!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<!--TgQPHd|[]--></tr>\n<tr class=\"cZCYO\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">\n<td class=\"cOeeGf\" colspan=\"undefined\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1.11111px solid rgb(220, 223, 229);\"><strong class=\"Yjhzub\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">Block Storage (RBD)<!--TgQPHd|[]--></strong><!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<td class=\"cOeeGf\" colspan=\"undefined\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1.11111px solid rgb(220, 223, 229);\">VMs &amp; DBs<!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<td class=\"cOeeGf\" colspan=\"undefined\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1.11111px solid rgb(220, 223, 229);\">Mounts virtual disks (RADOS Block Devices) for virtual machines or Kubernetes volumes.<!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<!--TgQPHd|[]--></tr>\n<tr class=\"cZCYO\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">\n<td class=\"cOeeGf\" colspan=\"undefined\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><strong class=\"Yjhzub\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">File System (CephFS)<!--TgQPHd|[]--></strong><!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<td class=\"cOeeGf\" colspan=\"undefined\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">Shared Access<!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<td class=\"cOeeGf\" colspan=\"undefined\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">A <span data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><a class=\"H23r4e\" href=\"https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/storage-ceph/8.0.0?topic=overview-ceph-file-system\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-hveid=\"CAAIJhAD\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 500; margin: 0px; text-decoration: underline 1px rgb(21, 88, 214); border-bottom: 0px rgb(21, 88, 214);\">POSIX-compliant</a><!--TgQPHd|[[&quot;https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/storage-ceph/8.0.0?topic\\u003doverview-ceph-file-system&quot;,null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[{&quot;1218&quot;:[16]}]],16,null,&quot;Ceph File System - IBM&quot;,&quot;Ceph File System (CephFS) is a distributed file system that integrates seamlessly with the Ceph storage architecture. By leveraging the Ceph RADOS (Reliable Autonomic Distributed Object Store), CephFS provides a scalable and robust file system interface, adhering to POSIX standards.&quot;,null,&quot;IBM&quot;,&quot;https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/faviconV2?url\\u003dhttps://www.ibm.com\\u0026client\\u003dAIM\\u0026size\\u003d64\\u0026type\\u003dFAVICON\\u0026enable_upscaled\\u003dtrue\\u0026fallback_opts\\u003dTYPE,SIZE,URL&quot;,[[1778331985474350,107769412,1729808051],null,null,null,null,[[0,35,127]]]]]--></span> distributed file system for standard folder-based access.<!--TgQPHd|[]--></td>\n<!--TgQPHd|[]--></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<!--TgQPHd|[[1,0,0,3,null,&quot;&quot;]]--></div>\n<div class=\"Fsg96\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-processed=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><!--TgQPHd|[]--></div>\n<div class=\"otQkpb\" role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"3\" data-animation-nesting=\"\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-processed=\"true\" data-sae=\"\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 600; margin: 24px 0px 12px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">Key Technologies<!--TgQPHd|[]--></div>\n<ul class=\"KsbFXc U6u95\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-processed=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 12px 0px 16px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">\n<li class=\"Z1qcYe\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-hveid=\"CAAILhAA\" data-complete=\"true\" data-sae=\"\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px 0px 12px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><span class=\"T286Pc\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><strong class=\"Yjhzub\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">CRUSH Algorithm:<!--TgQPHd|[]--></strong> Unlike traditional systems that use lookup tables, Ceph uses the <strong class=\"Yjhzub\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">CRUSH<!--TgQPHd|[]--></strong> (Controlled Replication Under Scalable Hashing) algorithm. This allows clients to calculate exactly where data is stored, removing bottlenecks and enabling <span data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><a class=\"H23r4e\" href=\"https://ceph.io/en/discover/technology/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-hveid=\"CAAILhAB\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 500; margin: 0px; text-decoration: underline 1px rgb(21, 88, 214); border-bottom: 0px rgb(21, 88, 214);\">limitless horizontal scaling</a><!--TgQPHd|[[&quot;https://ceph.io/en/discover/technology/&quot;,null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[{&quot;1218&quot;:[16]}]],16,null,&quot;Ceph.io — Technology&quot;,&quot;Whatever delivery framework you require, Ceph can be adapted and applied accordingly. Ceph provides a flexible, scalable, reliable and intelligently distributed solution for data storage, built on the unifying foundation of RADOS (Reliable Autonomic Distributed Object Store). By manipulating all storage as objects within RADOS, Ceph is able to easily distribute data throughout a cluster, even for block and file storage types.&quot;,&quot;https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q\\u003dtbn:ANd9GcR1zNcd_wzYmiyFNn3tcgvDAEx-7ds6-XK196EoC_xi3Z_xVv-D4kxIrGI23t0zWYNec_nTXX3dEkNjpIxsbw&quot;,&quot;Ceph&quot;,&quot;https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/faviconV2?url\\u003dhttps://ceph.io\\u0026client\\u003dAIM\\u0026size\\u003d64\\u0026type\\u003dFAVICON\\u0026enable_upscaled\\u003dtrue\\u0026fallback_opts\\u003dTYPE,SIZE,URL&quot;,[[1778331985474350,107769412,1729808051],null,null,null,null,[[0,41,127]]]]]--></span>.<!--TgQPHd|[]--></span><!--TgQPHd|[]--></li>\n<li class=\"Z1qcYe\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-hveid=\"CAAILhAC\" data-complete=\"true\" data-sae=\"\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px 0px 12px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><span class=\"T286Pc\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><strong class=\"Yjhzub\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">Self-Healing:<!--TgQPHd|[]--></strong> When hardware fails, the cluster automatically detects the loss and re-replicates data from healthy nodes to maintain the desired level of redundancy.<!--TgQPHd|[]--></span><!--TgQPHd|[]--></li>\n<li class=\"Z1qcYe\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-hveid=\"CAAILhAD\" data-complete=\"true\" data-sae=\"\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px 0px 12px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><span class=\"T286Pc\" data-sfc-cp=\"\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" aria-owns=\"action-menu-parent-container\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\"><strong class=\"Yjhzub\" data-sfc-root=\"c\" data-sfc-cb=\"\" data-complete=\"true\" data-copy-service-computed-style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0px; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 0px rgb(10, 10, 10);\">Commodity Hardware:<!--TgQPHd|[]--></strong> Ceph is designed to run on standard, non-proprietary servers, significantly reducing infrastructure costs</span></li>\n</ul>','2026-05-09 11:23:32','Storage',NULL,0,0),
(143,'Linux Security Hardening','linux-security-hardening','\n<h1>Linux Security Hardening</h1>\n\n<h2>Firewall</h2>\n<pre>\nsudo ufw enable\n</pre>\n','2026-05-09 11:23:32','Security',NULL,0,0),
(144,'Fail2Ban Intrusion Prevention','fail2ban-intrusion-prevention','<h1>Fail2Ban Setup</h1>\n<h2>Install Fail2Ban</h2>\n<h4>sudo apt install fail2ban<br><br>Check Fail2Ban service status<br><br>sudo systemctl status fail2ban</h4>','2026-05-09 11:23:32','Security',NULL,0,0),
(145,'AI Infrastructure on Linux','ai-infrastructure-linux','\n<h1>AI Infrastructure on Linux</h1>\n\n<h2>AI Stack</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>GPU Servers</li>\n<li>CUDA</li>\n<li>Vector DB</li>\n<li>LLM APIs</li>\n</ul>\n','2026-05-09 11:23:32','AI',NULL,0,0),
(146,'Ollama Local LLM Setup','ollama-local-llm-setup','<p>Below is a complete article that you can publish internally or externally for Linux administrators, developers, DevOps engineers, and AI architects.</p>\n<h1>Ollama AI: Complete Guide to Running Local Large Language Models (LLMs)</h1>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) has transformed how businesses automate customer support, generate content, analyze data, and build intelligent applications. Most AI applications rely on cloud-based Large Language Models (LLMs) such as GPT, Claude, and Gemini. While these services are powerful, they often involve recurring costs, data privacy concerns, internet dependency, and vendor lock-in.</p>\n<p>Ollama addresses these challenges by allowing organizations and individuals to run Large Language Models locally on their own infrastructure.</p>\n<p>This article explains what Ollama is, how it works, how to deploy it, and how applications can communicate with it through APIs.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>What is Ollama?</h1>\n<p>Ollama is an open-source platform designed to simplify the deployment and execution of Large Language Models (LLMs) on local servers, workstations, and cloud infrastructure.</p>\n<p>It acts as a lightweight AI inference engine that manages AI models and exposes REST APIs for applications.</p>\n<p>Instead of sending data to external AI providers, Ollama allows organizations to process AI requests entirely within their own infrastructure.</p>\n<p>Examples of supported models include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Llama 3</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Qwen</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>DeepSeek</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Gemma</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Mistral</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Phi</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>CodeLlama</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Why Use Ollama?</h1>\n<p>Organizations choose Ollama for several reasons:</p>\n<h3>Data Privacy</h3>\n<p>Sensitive business information never leaves the organization\'s environment.</p>\n<h3>Cost Reduction</h3>\n<p>No per-token or per-request charges.</p>\n<h3>Full Control</h3>\n<p>Complete control over models, updates, performance, and security.</p>\n<h3>Offline Operation</h3>\n<p>Works without internet access once models are downloaded.</p>\n<h3>API Integration</h3>\n<p>Can be integrated into existing applications using REST APIs.</p>\n<h3>Multi-Purpose AI</h3>\n<p>Can be used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Customer support</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Internal knowledge assistants</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Email generation</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Voice bots</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Chatbots</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Code generation</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Documentation assistance</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Contact center automation</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Understanding the AI Architecture</h1>\n<p>A common misconception is that Ollama itself is the AI model.</p>\n<p>Ollama is actually the runtime environment that loads and executes AI models.</p>\n<p>Architecture:</p>\n<p>User Application<br>|<br>|<br>REST API Request<br>|<br>v<br>+------------------+<br>| Ollama |<br>| AI Runtime Layer |<br>+------------------+<br>|<br>|<br>+------------------+<br>| AI Model |<br>| Qwen / Llama |<br>| DeepSeek |<br>+------------------+<br>|<br>v<br>AI Generated Response</p>\n<p>The application communicates with Ollama.</p>\n<p>Ollama communicates with the selected AI model.</p>\n<p>The model generates a response and returns it back through Ollama.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>How Ollama Works Internally</h1>\n<p>The workflow is as follows:</p>\n<p>Step 1:<br>User submits a question.</p>\n<p>Example:<br>\"What is Kubernetes?\"</p>\n<p>Step 2:<br>The application sends the question to Ollama.</p>\n<p>Step 3:<br>Ollama loads the configured model into memory.</p>\n<p>Step 4:<br>The model processes the prompt using billions of trained parameters.</p>\n<p>Step 5:<br>The model predicts the most appropriate next words.</p>\n<p>Step 6:<br>The generated response is returned.</p>\n<p>Example Response:<br>\"Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform...\"</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Ollama Components</h1>\n<h2>Runtime Engine</h2>\n<p>Responsible for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Loading models</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Memory management</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Request processing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>GPU utilization</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Model Storage</h2>\n<p>Stores downloaded AI models.</p>\n<p>Example location:</p>\n<p>/usr/share/ollama/.ollama/models</p>\n<h2>REST API</h2>\n<p>Allows external applications to communicate with Ollama.</p>\n<p>Default Port:</p>\n<p>11434</p>\n<h2>Inference Engine</h2>\n<p>Performs the actual AI calculations and response generation.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Installing Ollama on Ubuntu</h1>\n<h2>Step 1: Update System</h2>\n<p>sudo apt update<br>sudo apt upgrade -y</p>\n<h2>Step 2: Install Ollama</h2>\n<p>curl -fsSL <a href=\"https://ollama.com/install.sh\">https://ollama.com/install.sh</a> | sh</p>\n<p>Verify Installation:</p>\n<p>ollama --version</p>\n<p>Check Service:</p>\n<p>systemctl status ollama</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Starting Ollama</h1>\n<p>Enable Service:</p>\n<p>systemctl enable ollama</p>\n<p>Start Service:</p>\n<p>systemctl start ollama</p>\n<p>Verify:</p>\n<p>curl <a href=\"http://localhost:11434/api/tags\">http://localhost:11434/api/tags</a></p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Downloading AI Models</h1>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>ollama pull qwen3:8b</p>\n<p>or</p>\n<p>ollama pull llama3</p>\n<p>View Installed Models:</p>\n<p>ollama list</p>\n<p>Run Model:</p>\n<p>ollama run qwen3:8b</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Selecting the Right Model</h1>\n<p>Small Models (3B&ndash;8B)</p>\n<p>Advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Lower RAM usage</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Faster responses</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use Cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Chatbots</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>FAQ systems</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Internal assistants</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Medium Models (14B&ndash;32B)</p>\n<p>Advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Better reasoning</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Improved accuracy</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use Cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Enterprise AI</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Customer support</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Knowledge systems</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Large Models (70B+)</p>\n<p>Advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Highest quality</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Requirements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Multiple GPUs</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Large memory capacity</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Understanding Ollama APIs</h1>\n<p>Ollama exposes HTTP REST APIs.</p>\n<p>Default Endpoint:</p>\n<p>http://SERVER_IP:11434</p>\n<p>Applications communicate using JSON requests.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Generate API</h1>\n<p>Request:</p>\n<p>POST /api/generate</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>{<br>\"model\":\"qwen3:8b\",<br>\"prompt\":\"Explain Linux namespaces\"<br>}</p>\n<p>Response:</p>\n<p>{<br>\"response\":\"Linux namespaces provide...\"<br>}</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Chat API</h1>\n<p>Request:</p>\n<p>POST /api/chat</p>\n<p>{<br>\"model\":\"qwen3:8b\",<br>\"messages\":[<br>{<br>\"role\":\"user\",<br>\"content\":\"Explain Docker\"<br>}<br>]<br>}</p>\n<p>Response:</p>\n<p>{<br>\"message\":{<br>\"role\":\"assistant\",<br>\"content\":\"Docker is a container platform...\"<br>}<br>}</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Communication Between Applications and Ollama</h1>\n<p>Application Architecture</p>\n<p>Web Application<br>|<br>|<br>HTTP Request<br>|<br>v<br>Ollama API<br>|<br>v<br>LLM Model<br>|<br>v<br>Response</p>\n<p>The application acts as the client.</p>\n<p>Ollama acts as the AI server.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Example Workflow</h1>\n<p>User enters:</p>\n<p>\"Explain MySQL Replication\"</p>\n<p>Application sends:</p>\n<p>POST /api/chat</p>\n<p>Ollama processes request.</p>\n<p>Model generates response.</p>\n<p>Application displays result.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Integration Example Using PHP</h1>\n<p>The response can be displayed directly in the web interface.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Integration Example Using Python</h1>\n<p>import requests</p>\n<p>payload = {<br>\"model\":\"qwen3:8b\",<br>\"messages\":[<br>{<br>\"role\":\"user\",<br>\"content\":\"Explain Kubernetes\"<br>}<br>]<br>}</p>\n<p>response = requests.post(<br>\"<a href=\"http://localhost:11434/api/chat\">http://localhost:11434/api/chat</a>\",<br>json=payload<br>)</p>\n<p>print(response.json())</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Integrating with Existing Applications</h1>\n<p>Ollama can be integrated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>PHP Applications</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Python Applications</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Java Applications</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Node.js Applications</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>React Applications</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Angular Applications</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Mobile Applications</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>CRM Platforms</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ERP Systems</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Contact Center Platforms</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Enterprise AI Architecture</h1>\n<p>Users<br>|<br>Web Portal<br>|<br>API Gateway<br>|<br>Ollama Cluster<br>|<br>Qwen / Llama<br>|<br>Knowledge Base</p>\n<p>This architecture supports centralized AI services for multiple applications.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Building an Internal Knowledge Assistant</h1>\n<p>Organizations can combine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Ollama</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Vector Database</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Company Documents</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This architecture is known as Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).</p>\n<p>Documents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>SOPs</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>PDFs</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Manuals</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Policies</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Technical Guides</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The AI assistant can answer questions using internal company knowledge.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>User:<br>\"How do I restart the mail cluster?\"</p>\n<p>AI:<br>Returns the organization\'s documented procedure.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Using Ollama for Voice Bots</h1>\n<p>Voice Bot Flow</p>\n<p>Caller<br>|<br>Speech-to-Text<br>|<br>Ollama<br>|<br>Response<br>|<br>Text-to-Speech<br>|<br>Caller</p>\n<p>Components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>FreeSWITCH</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Speech Recognition</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ollama</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Text-to-Speech Engine</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is commonly used in AI-powered contact centers.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Security Best Practices</h1>\n<p>Never expose Ollama directly to the Internet.</p>\n<p>Recommended Architecture:</p>\n<p>Internet<br>|<br>Nginx<br>|<br>Authentication<br>|<br>Ollama</p>\n<p>Security Controls:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>HTTPS</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>JWT Authentication</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rate Limiting</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>API Logging</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Firewall Rules</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Performance Optimization</h1>\n<p>For Production Deployments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Use SSD/NVMe storage</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Enable GPU acceleration</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Increase RAM capacity</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Use load balancing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Deploy multiple AI nodes</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<h2>Load Balancer<br>|</h2>\n<p>| | |<br>AI-1 AI-2 AI-3</p>\n<p>This improves scalability and availability.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Advantages of Ollama</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>No API costs</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Data privacy</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Offline operation</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fast deployment</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Open-source ecosystem</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Enterprise integration</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Multiple model support</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Limitations</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Requires significant hardware for large models</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Model quality depends on selected LLM</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>GPU resources may be required for high performance</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Local maintenance responsibility</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Conclusion</h1>\n<p>Ollama provides a powerful and practical way to deploy Large Language Models within an organization\'s own infrastructure. By exposing simple REST APIs, it allows existing applications, chatbots, voice bots, contact centers, and enterprise systems to leverage AI capabilities without relying on external cloud providers.</p>\n<p>For organizations seeking privacy, cost control, customization, and complete ownership of their AI platform, Ollama is one of the most effective solutions currently available.</p>\n<p>Whether building an internal AI assistant, customer support chatbot, AI-powered contact center, or enterprise knowledge platform, Ollama offers a scalable foundation for deploying modern AI applications.</p>\n<p>This article is suitable for publication on a company blog, LinkedIn article, technical wiki, Medium, or internal knowledge base. It provides both conceptual understanding and practical implementation guidance.</p>','2026-05-09 11:23:32','AI',NULL,0,0),
(147,'LangChain AI Workflows','langchain-ai-workflows','\n<h1>LangChain Basics</h1>\n\n<h2>LLM Chains</h2>\n<p>\nLangChain helps build AI workflows.\n</p>\n','2026-05-09 11:23:32','AI',NULL,0,0),
(148,'Vector Database Fundamentals','vector-database-fundamentals','\n<h1>Vector Database</h1>\n\n<h2>What is Vector DB?</h2>\n<p>\nStores embeddings for AI search.\n</p>\n','2026-05-09 11:23:32','AI',NULL,0,0),
(149,'Asterisk PBX Setup','asterisk-pbx-setup','\n<h1>Asterisk PBX Setup</h1>\n\n<h2>Install Asterisk</h2>\n<pre>\nsudo apt install asterisk\n</pre>\n','2026-05-09 11:23:32','VoIP',NULL,0,0),
(150,'FreeSWITCH Installation','freeswitch-installation','<h1>FreeSWITCH Installation</h1>\n<h2>FreeSWITCH</h2>\n<p><strong>FreeSWITCH</strong> is a high-performance, open-source telecommunications platform used to build voice, video, messaging, conferencing, and real-time communication applications. It is often considered a software PBX, softswitch, and media server in a single platform.</p>\n<p>FreeSWITCH</p>\n<h3>What FreeSWITCH Can Do</h3>\n<p>FreeSWITCH is commonly used for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>SIP-based VoIP systems</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Contact Centers / CCaaS platforms</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>IVR (Interactive Voice Response)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Voice Broadcasting</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Audio &amp; Video Conferencing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Call Recording</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>WebRTC Integration</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>AI Voice Bots</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Call Routing and Switching</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SMS and Chat Integration</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Carrier-grade Telecom Applications</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h3>FreeSWITCH Architecture</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">SIP Phones\n     |\n     |\n  FreeSWITCH\n     |\n-------------------------\n|          |            |\nPSTN    SIP Trunks    AI Bots\nGateway    |          |\n            CRM      ASR/TTS\n</code></pre>\n<p>Core Components:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sofia SIP</td>\n<td>SIP signaling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RTP Engine</td>\n<td>Audio/Video streaming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dialplan</td>\n<td>Call routing logic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESL (Event Socket Library)</td>\n<td>External application control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mod_conference</td>\n<td>Audio conferencing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mod_callcenter</td>\n<td>Call center queues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mod_voicemail</td>\n<td>Voicemail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mod_lua / mod_python</td>\n<td>Custom scripting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr>\n<h3>FreeSWITCH vs Asterisk</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>FreeSWITCH</th>\n<th>Asterisk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Scalability</td>\n<td>Excellent</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multi-threading</td>\n<td>Native</td>\n<td>Limited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large Contact Centers</td>\n<td>Excellent</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video Support</td>\n<td>Better</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WebRTC</td>\n<td>Excellent</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High Concurrent Calls</td>\n<td>Excellent</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI Integration</td>\n<td>Excellent</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For large CCaaS deployments, many organizations choose FreeSWITCH because of its event-driven architecture and ability to handle thousands of concurrent calls.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3>Common Ports</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Port</th>\n<th>Protocol</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>5060</td>\n<td>SIP UDP/TCP</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5061</td>\n<td>SIP TLS</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16384-32768</td>\n<td>RTP Media</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8021</td>\n<td>Event Socket (ESL)</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7443</td>\n<td>WebRTC/WSS</td>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr>\n<h3>Installation on Ubuntu 22.04/24.04</h3>\n<h4>1. Update Server</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">apt update\napt upgrade -y\n</code></pre>\n<h4>2. Install Dependencies</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">apt install -y gnupg2 wget lsb-release\n</code></pre>\n<h4>3. Add FreeSWITCH Repository</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">wget -O - https://files.freeswitch.org/repo/deb/debian-release/fsstretch-archive-keyring.asc | apt-key add -\n</code></pre>\n<p>Create repository:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">echo \"deb http://files.freeswitch.org/repo/deb/debian-release/ $(lsb_release -sc) main\" &gt; /etc/apt/sources.list.d/freeswitch.list\n</code></pre>\n<h4>4. Install</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">apt update\n\napt install -y \\\nfreeswitch-meta-all \\\nfreeswitch-lang-en \\\nfreeswitch-mod-conference \\\nfreeswitch-mod-callcenter\n</code></pre>\n<h4>5. Start Service</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">systemctl enable freeswitch\nsystemctl start freeswitch\nsystemctl status freeswitch\n</code></pre>\n<h4>6. Connect to CLI</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">fs_cli\n</code></pre>\n<p>Useful commands:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">status\nsofia status\nshow channels\nshow calls\nreloadxml\n</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h3>Call Center Modules</h3>\n<p>For CCaaS deployments, the most important modules are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">mod_sofia\nmod_callcenter\nmod_conference\nmod_event_socket\nmod_lua\nmod_xml_curl\nmod_av\nmod_verto\nmod_rtc\n</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h3>AI Voice Bot Integration</h3>\n<p>FreeSWITCH integrates well with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>OpenAI Realtime API</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Deepgram</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Google Speech-to-Text</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Amazon Polly</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Azure Speech Service</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is why many AI-powered contact centers use FreeSWITCH as the telephony engine.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3>Typical Production Deployment</h3>\n<p>Since you\'re working on AI-CCaaS and contact-center platforms, a production architecture would typically be:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">SBC/Kamailio\n      |\n      v\n FreeSWITCH Cluster\n      |\n  Redis Queue\n      |\n Application Layer\n      |\n MySQL Cluster\n      |\n AI Services\n</code></pre>\n<p>Using:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>FreeSWITCH for telephony</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Kamailio for SIP load balancing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Redis for queues</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>MySQL for CDR and application data</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This architecture can support thousands of concurrent calls when properly tuned on servers like your 48-core, 96GB RAM environment.</p>','2026-05-09 11:23:32','VoIP',NULL,0,0),
(151,'Understanding SIP Protocol: Architecture, Call Flow, and Call Handling Explained','understanding-SIP-Protocol: Architecture-CallFlow-and-CallHandling Explained','<p>Here\'s a comprehensive article suitable for Linux Administrators, VoIP Engineers, Telecom Engineers, CCaaS Architects, and anyone learning SIP-based communications.</p>\n<h1>Understanding SIP Protocol: Architecture, Call Flow, and Call Handling Explained</h1>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>The Session Initiation Protocol (SIP) is the foundation of modern Voice over IP (VoIP) communications. It is used by IP phones, softphones, contact centers, video conferencing platforms, VoIP gateways, and communication platforms such as Asterisk, FreeSWITCH, and cloud-based communication systems.</p>\n<p>Whenever someone makes a VoIP call, SIP is typically responsible for establishing, modifying, and terminating the communication session.</p>\n<p>Understanding SIP is essential for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>VoIP Engineers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Linux Administrators</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Telecom Engineers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Contact Center Architects</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>FreeSWITCH Administrators</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Asterisk Administrators</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Network Engineers</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This article explains SIP from the ground up, including architecture, signaling, call flows, media handling, troubleshooting, and real-world deployments.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>What is SIP?</h1>\n<p>SIP (Session Initiation Protocol) is an application-layer signaling protocol used to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Establish calls</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Modify calls</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Terminate calls</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Manage communication sessions</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>SIP itself does not carry voice.</p>\n<p>Instead, SIP is responsible for signaling and session control.</p>\n<p>Actual voice communication is typically carried using RTP (Real-time Transport Protocol).</p>\n<p>Think of SIP as a telephone operator that:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Finds the destination.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Rings the destination.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Connects both parties.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Terminates the session when finished.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP vs RTP</h1>\n<p>Many people mistakenly believe SIP carries voice.</p>\n<p>In reality:</p>\n<p>SIP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Call setup</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Call control</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Call termination</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>RTP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Audio transmission</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Video transmission</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>Caller Phone<br>|<br>| SIP<br>|<br>PBX<br>|<br>| SIP<br>|<br>Receiver Phone</p>\n<p>After call establishment:</p>\n<p>Caller Phone<br>&lt;---- RTP Audio ----&gt;<br>Receiver Phone</p>\n<p>The voice flows directly through RTP.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Architecture</h1>\n<p>Basic Components:</p>\n<p>+----------------+<br>| SIP User Agent |<br>+----------------+<br>|<br>|<br>+----------------+<br>| SIP Server |<br>+----------------+<br>|<br>|<br>+----------------+<br>| SIP User Agent |<br>+----------------+</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Components</h1>\n<h2>User Agent Client (UAC)</h2>\n<p>Initiates calls.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>IP Phones</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Softphones</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Mobile Apps</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>Alice calls Bob.</p>\n<p>Alice\'s phone acts as UAC.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>User Agent Server (UAS)</h2>\n<p>Receives calls.</p>\n<p>Bob\'s phone becomes UAS.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>SIP Proxy Server</h2>\n<p>Responsible for routing SIP messages.</p>\n<p>Functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Registration</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Routing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Authentication</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Load balancing</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Kamailio</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>OpenSIPS</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h2>SIP Registrar</h2>\n<p>Maintains user location information.</p>\n<p>Stores:</p>\n<p>User<br>IP Address<br>Port</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>1001 &rarr; 192.168.1.100</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>SIP Redirect Server</h2>\n<p>Returns destination information without forwarding the call.</p>\n<p>Used in some carrier environments.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP URI</h1>\n<p>Like an email address.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>sip:<a href=\"mailto:1001@company.com\">1001@company.com</a></p>\n<p>sip:support@company.com</p>\n<p>sip:user@domain.com</p>\n<p>Structure:</p>\n<p>sip:user@domain</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Ports</h1>\n<p>Default Ports:</p>\n<p>UDP 5060</p>\n<p>TCP 5060</p>\n<p>TLS 5061</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>sip.company.com:5060</p>\n<p>sip.company.com:5061</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Common SIP Methods</h1>\n<h2>REGISTER</h2>\n<p>Registers endpoint location.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>REGISTER sip:company.com</p>\n<p>Purpose:</p>\n<p>\"Here is my current IP address.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>INVITE</h2>\n<p>Initiates a call.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>INVITE sip:<a href=\"mailto:1002@company.com\">1002@company.com</a></p>\n<p>Purpose:</p>\n<p>\"I want to talk to this user.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>ACK</h2>\n<p>Acknowledges successful call setup.</p>\n<p>Purpose:</p>\n<p>\"Call connected successfully.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>BYE</h2>\n<p>Terminates a call.</p>\n<p>Purpose:</p>\n<p>\"End the call.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>CANCEL</h2>\n<p>Cancels call before answer.</p>\n<p>Purpose:</p>\n<p>\"Stop ringing.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>OPTIONS</h2>\n<p>Capability check.</p>\n<p>Purpose:</p>\n<p>\"Are you alive?\"</p>\n<p>Often used for monitoring.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Registration Process</h1>\n<p>IP Phone Startup:</p>\n<p>Phone<br>|<br>REGISTER<br>|<br>PBX</p>\n<p>PBX<br>|<br>200 OK<br>|<br>Phone</p>\n<p>Result:</p>\n<p>User location stored.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>1001 &rarr; 10.10.10.50</p>\n<p>Now incoming calls can be routed.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Call Flow</h1>\n<p>Assume:</p>\n<p>Alice = 1001</p>\n<p>Bob = 1002</p>\n<p>Alice calls Bob.</p>\n<p>Step 1:</p>\n<p>1001 sends INVITE</p>\n<p>INVITE sip:<a href=\"mailto:1002@company.com\">1002@company.com</a></p>\n<hr>\n<p>Step 2:</p>\n<p>PBX responds:</p>\n<p>100 Trying</p>\n<p>Meaning:</p>\n<p>\"I received the request.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Step 3:</p>\n<p>PBX forwards INVITE to Bob.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Step 4:</p>\n<p>Bob\'s phone rings.</p>\n<p>PBX returns:</p>\n<p>180 Ringing</p>\n<p>Meaning:</p>\n<p>\"Destination is ringing.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Step 5:</p>\n<p>Bob answers.</p>\n<p>PBX returns:</p>\n<p>200 OK</p>\n<p>Meaning:</p>\n<p>\"Call accepted.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Step 6:</p>\n<p>Alice sends ACK.</p>\n<p>Call becomes active.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Step 7:</p>\n<p>RTP begins.</p>\n<p>Voice starts flowing.</p>\n<p>Alice<br>&lt;---- RTP ----&gt;<br>Bob</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Message Example</h1>\n<p>INVITE sip:<a href=\"mailto:1002@company.com\">1002@company.com</a> SIP/2.0</p>\n<p>Via: SIP/2.0/UDP 192.168.1.10</p>\n<p>From: <a href=\"sip:1001@company.com\">sip:1001@company.com</a></p>\n<p>To: <a href=\"sip:1002@company.com\">sip:1002@company.com</a></p>\n<p>Call-ID: abc123</p>\n<p>CSeq: 1 INVITE</p>\n<p>This message uniquely identifies the call.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SDP (Session Description Protocol)</h1>\n<p>SIP uses SDP to negotiate media.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>v=0</p>\n<p>m=audio 20000 RTP/AVP 0 8 18</p>\n<p>This tells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>RTP Port</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Codec Options</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Media Type</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Codec Negotiation</h1>\n<p>Caller supports:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>G711</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>G729</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Opus</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Receiver supports:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>G711</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Opus</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common codec selected:</p>\n<p>G711</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>RTP Audio Flow</h1>\n<p>After SIP completes:</p>\n<p>SIP stops controlling audio.</p>\n<p>RTP carries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Voice</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Video</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>DTMF</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common RTP Range:</p>\n<p>10000-20000</p>\n<p>16384-32768</p>\n<p>Depends on PBX configuration.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Authentication</h1>\n<p>Uses Digest Authentication.</p>\n<p>Phone sends:</p>\n<p>REGISTER</p>\n<p>Server responds:</p>\n<p>401 Unauthorized</p>\n<p>Phone retries with:</p>\n<p>Username</p>\n<p>Realm</p>\n<p>Hash</p>\n<p>Password</p>\n<p>If valid:</p>\n<p>200 OK</p>\n<p>Registration succeeds.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>NAT Challenges</h1>\n<p>Many SIP deployments sit behind routers.</p>\n<p>Problems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>One-way audio</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Registration failures</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RTP issues</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Solutions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>STUN</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TURN</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>ICE</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SBC</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Trunking</h1>\n<p>Traditional PSTN:</p>\n<p>Phone &rarr; Telco</p>\n<p>Modern SIP:</p>\n<p>Phone &rarr; PBX &rarr; SIP Provider &rarr; PSTN</p>\n<p>Benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Lower cost</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Global reach</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Scalability</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP in Contact Centers</h1>\n<p>Customer<br>|<br>SIP Trunk<br>|<br>PBX<br>|<br>Queue<br>|<br>Agent</p>\n<p>Calls are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Queued</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Recorded</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Routed</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Monitored</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP and FreeSWITCH</h1>\n<p>FreeSWITCH commonly uses:</p>\n<p>mod_sofia</p>\n<p>Functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Registration</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Call Routing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SIP Trunks</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Authentication</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Commands:</p>\n<p>sofia status</p>\n<p>show registrations</p>\n<p>show channels</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP and Asterisk</h1>\n<p>Asterisk supports:</p>\n<p>chan_sip (legacy)</p>\n<p>PJSIP (modern)</p>\n<p>Common Commands:</p>\n<p>pjsip show endpoints</p>\n<p>pjsip show registrations</p>\n<p>core show channels</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>SIP Troubleshooting</h1>\n<h2>Check Registration</h2>\n<p>FreeSWITCH:</p>\n<p>sofia status profile internal reg</p>\n<p>Asterisk:</p>\n<p>pjsip show contacts</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Packet Capture</h2>\n<p>tcpdump -i any udp port 5060</p>\n<p>Analyze:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>INVITE</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>REGISTER</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>BYE</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>OPTIONS</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h2>SIP Trace</h2>\n<p>sngrep</p>\n<p>One of the most powerful SIP debugging tools.</p>\n<p>Shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Call flow</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SIP messages</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>RTP information</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Security Best Practices</h1>\n<p>Block unauthorized access.</p>\n<p>Use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>TLS</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SRTP</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fail2Ban</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SBC</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>IP restrictions</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prevent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>SIP scanning</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Toll fraud</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Registration attacks</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Brute-force attacks</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h1>Real-World AI Contact Center Architecture</h1>\n<p>Customer<br>|<br>SIP Trunk<br>|<br>Kamailio SBC<br>|<br>FreeSWITCH Cluster<br>|<br>AI Voice Bot<br>|<br>Speech Recognition<br>|<br>LLM (Ollama/OpenAI)<br>|<br>Text-to-Speech<br>|<br>Customer</p>\n<p>This architecture powers modern AI-enabled contact centers and conversational AI systems.</p>\n<hr>\n<h1>Conclusion</h1>\n<p>SIP is the signaling backbone of modern VoIP communications. It is responsible for establishing, managing, and terminating communication sessions, while RTP carries the actual media streams.</p>\n<p>Understanding SIP registration, INVITE transactions, RTP flow, codec negotiation, authentication, and troubleshooting is essential for anyone managing VoIP platforms such as FreeSWITCH, Asterisk, Kamailio, OpenSIPS, or AI-powered contact centers.</p>\n<p>Mastering SIP provides the foundation for building scalable voice, video, and unified communication systems in today\'s cloud and AI-driven environments.</p>\n<p>For your AI-CCaaS and FreeSWITCH environment, the next logical deep-dive article would be <strong>\"SIP Call Journey: End-to-End Flow from Customer PSTN Call &rarr; SIP Trunk &rarr; Kamailio &rarr; FreeSWITCH &rarr; Queue &rarr; Agent &rarr; Recording &rarr; AI Analytics\"</strong>, including actual SIP packets, RTP streams, NAT traversal, and call recording architecture.</p>','2026-05-09 11:23:32','VoIP',NULL,0,0),
(152,'WebRTC Infrastructure','webrtc-infrastructure','<h1>WebRTC Infrastructure</h1>\n<h2>Real Time Communication</h2>\n<p>Browser based voice and video.</p>\n<p>Test.</p>','2026-05-09 11:23:32','VoIP',NULL,0,0),
(153,'Linux Boot Process','linux-boot-process','<h1>Linux Boot Process</h1>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>The Linux boot process is the sequence of steps that initializes the system hardware and loads the Linux operating system into memory.</p>\n<h2>Stages of Linux Boot Process</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>BIOS / UEFI</li>\n<li>Boot Loader (GRUB)</li>\n<li>Kernel Initialization</li>\n<li>initramfs</li>\n<li>systemd / init</li>\n<li>Runlevel / Target</li>\n<li>User Login</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>1. BIOS / UEFI</h2>\n<p>The BIOS or UEFI firmware starts first when the system powers on. It performs hardware initialization and POST (Power-On Self Test).</p>\n<h2>2. Boot Loader (GRUB)</h2>\n<p>GRUB loads the Linux kernel into memory.</p>\n<pre>/boot/grub/grub.cfg\n</pre>\n<h2>Common GRUB Commands</h2>\n<pre>update-grub\ngrub-install /dev/sda\n</pre>\n<h2>3. Linux Kernel Initialization</h2>\n<p>The Linux kernel initializes CPU, memory, drivers and hardware devices.</p>\n<h2>Kernel Information</h2>\n<pre>uname -r\n</pre>\n<h2>4. initramfs</h2>\n<p>initramfs provides temporary root filesystem required during boot.</p>\n<h2>Regenerate initramfs</h2>\n<pre>update-initramfs -u\n</pre>\n<h2>5. systemd Initialization</h2>\n<p>systemd becomes PID 1 and starts system services.</p>\n<h2>Check systemd</h2>\n<pre>systemctl status\n</pre>\n<h2>6. Runlevels / Targets</h2>\n<p>Modern Linux distributions use systemd targets instead of runlevels.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<th>Target</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>multi-user.target</td>\n<td>CLI mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>graphical.target</td>\n<td>GUI mode</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Change Default Target</h2>\n<pre>systemctl set-default multi-user.target\n</pre>\n<h2>7. User Login</h2>\n<p>After services start successfully, login prompt appears.</p>\n<h2>Troubleshooting Boot Issues</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check GRUB configuration</li>\n<li>Review journal logs</li>\n<li>Boot into rescue mode</li>\n<li>Verify filesystem integrity</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Useful Commands</h2>\n<pre>journalctl -b\nsystemctl list-units\ndmesg\nlsblk\n</pre>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Understanding the Linux boot process helps administrators troubleshoot startup problems and optimize system initialization.</p>','2026-05-09 11:46:48','Linux',NULL,0,0),
(154,'Linux File System Evolution Based on Linux Variants','linux-file-system-evolution-linux-variants','\n<h1>Linux File System Evolution Based on Linux Variants</h1>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>\nLinux file systems have evolved significantly across different Linux distributions and kernel generations.\nDifferent Linux variants adopted file systems optimized for performance, scalability, reliability and enterprise workloads.\n</p>\n\n<h2>What is a File System?</h2>\n\n<p>\nA file system controls how data is stored, organized and retrieved from storage devices.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Linux File System Hierarchy</h2>\n\n<pre>\n/\n├── bin\n├── boot\n├── dev\n├── etc\n├── home\n├── lib\n├── opt\n├── proc\n├── root\n├── run\n├── sbin\n├── tmp\n├── usr\n├── var\n</pre>\n\n<h2>Evolution of Linux File Systems</h2>\n\n<table>\n\n<tr>\n<th>File System</th>\n<th>Linux Variant</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Ext</td>\n<td>Early Linux</td>\n<td>First Linux filesystem</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Ext2</td>\n<td>RedHat / Debian</td>\n<td>Stable non-journaling FS</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Ext3</td>\n<td>Enterprise Linux</td>\n<td>Journaling support</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Ext4</td>\n<td>Ubuntu / CentOS / Debian</td>\n<td>Modern default filesystem</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>XFS</td>\n<td>RHEL / Rocky Linux</td>\n<td>High performance enterprise FS</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Btrfs</td>\n<td>SUSE / Fedora</td>\n<td>Snapshots and advanced features</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>ZFS</td>\n<td>Ubuntu / FreeBSD</td>\n<td>Enterprise storage</td>\n</tr>\n\n</table>\n\n<h2>1. Ext File System</h2>\n\n<p>\nThe original Extended File System introduced for Linux systems.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Limitations</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Limited file size</li>\n\n<li>No journaling</li>\n\n<li>Performance limitations</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>2. Ext2 File System</h2>\n\n<p>\nSecond Extended File System became widely used in Linux distributions.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Features</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Improved performance</li>\n\n<li>Large partition support</li>\n\n<li>Better stability</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Create Ext2 Filesystem</h2>\n\n<pre>\nmkfs.ext2 /dev/sdb1\n</pre>\n\n<h2>3. Ext3 File System</h2>\n\n<p>\nExt3 introduced journaling to improve crash recovery.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Advantages</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Fast recovery after reboot</li>\n\n<li>Backward compatible with Ext2</li>\n\n<li>Improved reliability</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Create Ext3 Filesystem</h2>\n\n<pre>\nmkfs.ext3 /dev/sdb1\n</pre>\n\n<h2>4. Ext4 File System</h2>\n\n<p>\nExt4 is the most commonly used filesystem in modern Linux distributions.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Features</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Large file support</li>\n\n<li>Better performance</li>\n\n<li>Delayed allocation</li>\n\n<li>Faster fsck</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Create Ext4 Filesystem</h2>\n\n<pre>\nmkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1\n</pre>\n\n<h2>5. XFS File System</h2>\n\n<p>\nXFS is optimized for enterprise and high-performance workloads.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Used In</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>RHEL</li>\n\n<li>Rocky Linux</li>\n\n<li>AlmaLinux</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Advantages</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Excellent scalability</li>\n\n<li>High I/O performance</li>\n\n<li>Large filesystem support</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Create XFS Filesystem</h2>\n\n<pre>\nmkfs.xfs /dev/sdb1\n</pre>\n\n<h2>6. Btrfs File System</h2>\n\n<p>\nBtrfs provides modern features such as snapshots and compression.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Used In</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>openSUSE</li>\n\n<li>Fedora</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Features</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Snapshots</li>\n\n<li>Compression</li>\n\n<li>RAID support</li>\n\n<li>Self-healing</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Create Btrfs Filesystem</h2>\n\n<pre>\nmkfs.btrfs /dev/sdb1\n</pre>\n\n<h2>7. ZFS File System</h2>\n\n<p>\nZFS is an advanced enterprise-grade filesystem designed for massive storage systems.\n</p>\n\n<h2>Features</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Data integrity</li>\n\n<li>Snapshots</li>\n\n<li>Deduplication</li>\n\n<li>Compression</li>\n\n<li>RAID-Z</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Linux Variants and Default File Systems</h2>\n\n<table>\n\n<tr>\n<th>Distribution</th>\n<th>Default Filesystem</th>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Ubuntu</td>\n<td>Ext4</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Debian</td>\n<td>Ext4</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>RHEL 9</td>\n<td>XFS</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Rocky Linux</td>\n<td>XFS</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Fedora</td>\n<td>Btrfs</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>openSUSE</td>\n<td>Btrfs</td>\n</tr>\n\n</table>\n\n<h2>Check Filesystem Type</h2>\n\n<pre>\nlsblk -f\n</pre>\n\n<h2>Mount Filesystem</h2>\n\n<pre>\nmount /dev/sdb1 /mnt\n</pre>\n\n<h2>Check Disk Usage</h2>\n\n<pre>\ndf -h\n</pre>\n\n<h2>Filesystem Repair</h2>\n\n<pre>\nfsck /dev/sdb1\n</pre>\n\n<h2>Modern Enterprise Trends</h2>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>XFS for enterprise workloads</li>\n\n<li>Btrfs for snapshot-based systems</li>\n\n<li>ZFS for large storage clusters</li>\n\n<li>Ext4 for general purpose Linux systems</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n\n<p>\nLinux file systems evolved from simple storage management to highly scalable enterprise-grade technologies supporting cloud, virtualization and AI infrastructure workloads.\n</p>\n','2026-05-09 11:53:45','Linux',NULL,0,0),
(155,'Apache AGE - PostgreSQL - Complete Docker-Based Monitoring Setup','apache-age-postgresql-grafana-setup','<h1>Apache AGE - PostgreSQL - Complete Docker-Based Monitoring Setup</h1>\n<p data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"354\">This setup uses Docker + Docker Compose for:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"446\">\n<li data-section-id=\"961ble\" data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"367\">Prometheus</li>\n<li data-section-id=\"wkmoms\" data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"377\">Grafana</li>\n<li data-section-id=\"1dbyho5\" data-start=\"378\" data-end=\"392\">Alertmanager</li>\n<li data-section-id=\"1j3uiqx\" data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"399\">Loki</li>\n<li data-section-id=\"3v1v9b\" data-start=\"400\" data-end=\"419\">postgres_exporter</li>\n<li data-section-id=\"e843l0\" data-start=\"420\" data-end=\"435\">node_exporter</li>\n<li data-section-id=\"1po6ho8\" data-start=\"436\" data-end=\"446\">promtail</li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-section-id=\"14st1nb\" data-start=\"453\" data-end=\"492\">PART 1 &mdash; Install Docker (ALL Servers)</h1>\n<p data-start=\"494\" data-end=\"501\">Run on:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"502\" data-end=\"535\">\n<li data-section-id=\"1lq49h8\" data-start=\"502\" data-end=\"518\">Monitoring Dashboard Server - 192.168.0.19</li>\n<li data-section-id=\"efoni3\" data-start=\"519\" data-end=\"535\">AGE+PostgreSQL - 192.168.0.20</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-section-id=\"9dohmo\" data-start=\"542\" data-end=\"571\">Ubuntu Docker Installation</h2>\n<div class=\"relative w-full mt-4 mb-1\">\n<div class=\"\">\n<div class=\"relative\">\n<div class=\"h-full min-h-0 min-w-0\">\n<div class=\"h-full min-h-0 min-w-0\">\n<div class=\"border border-token-border-light border-radius-3xl corner-superellipse/1.1 rounded-3xl\">\n<div class=\"h-full w-full border-radius-3xl bg-token-bg-elevated-secondary corner-superellipse/1.1 overflow-clip rounded-3xl lxnfua_clipPathFallback\">\n<div class=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-4 top-12 bottom-4\">\n<div class=\"pointer-events-none sticky z-40 shrink-0 z-1!\">&nbsp;</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>apt update</p>\n<p>apt install -y \\<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; ca-certificates \\<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; curl \\<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; gnupg \\<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; lsb-release</p>\n<h4>Add Docker Repository</h4>\n<p>mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings</p>\n<p>curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | \\<br>gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg<br><br></p>\n<p>echo \\<br>&nbsp; \"deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) \\<br>&nbsp; signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg] \\<br>&nbsp; https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu \\<br>&nbsp; $(lsb_release -cs) stable\" | \\<br>&nbsp; tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list &gt; /dev/null</p>\n<h3>Install Docker Engine</h3>\n<p>apt update</p>\n<p>apt install -y \\<br>&nbsp; docker-ce \\<br>&nbsp; docker-ce-cli \\<br>&nbsp; containerd.io \\<br>&nbsp; docker-buildx-plugin \\<br>&nbsp; docker-compose-plugin</p>\n<h4>Verify docker versions</h4>\n<p>docker --version<br>docker compose version</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<h3>PART 2 &mdash; Monitoring Dashboard Server</h3>\n<h3 data-section-id=\"1yspmn5\" data-start=\"1410\" data-end=\"1441\">Create Monitoring Directories</h3>\n<div class=\"relative w-full mt-4 mb-1\">\n<div class=\"\">\n<div class=\"relative\">\n<div class=\"h-full min-h-0 min-w-0\">\n<div class=\"h-full min-h-0 min-w-0\">\n<div class=\"border border-token-border-light border-radius-3xl corner-superellipse/1.1 rounded-3xl\">\n<div class=\"h-full w-full border-radius-3xl bg-token-bg-elevated-secondary corner-superellipse/1.1 overflow-clip rounded-3xl lxnfua_clipPathFallback\">\n<div class=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-4 top-12 bottom-4\">\n<div class=\"pointer-events-none sticky z-40 shrink-0 z-1!\">\n<div class=\"sticky bg-token-border-light\">&nbsp;</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"relative\">\n<div class=\"\">\n<div class=\"relative z-0 flex max-w-full\">\n<div id=\"code-block-viewer\" class=\"q9tKkq_viewer cm-editor z-10 light:cm-light dark:cm-light flex h-full w-full flex-col items-stretch ͼd ͼr\" dir=\"ltr\">\n<div class=\"cm-scroller\">\n<pre class=\"cm-content q9tKkq_readonly m-0\"><code><span class=\"ͼl\">mkdir</span> <span class=\"ͼn\">-p</span> /opt/monitoring<br><br><span class=\"ͼl\">cd</span> /opt/monitoring<br><br></code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"ͼl\">mkdir</span> <span class=\"ͼn\">-p</span> prometheus<br><span class=\"ͼl\">mkdir</span> <span class=\"ͼn\">-p</span> grafana<br><span class=\"ͼl\">mkdir</span> <span class=\"ͼn\">-p</span> loki<br><span class=\"ͼl\">mkdir</span> <span class=\"ͼn\">-p</span> alertmanager</p>\n<h3>Create Docker Compose</h3>\n<p>Create docker-compose file</p>\n<p>vim /opt/monitoring/docker-compose.yml</p>\n<p>version: \'3.9\'</p>\n<p>services:</p>\n<p>&nbsp; prometheus:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; image: prom/prometheus:latest<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; container_name: prometheus<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; restart: unless-stopped</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; volumes:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - ./prometheus/prometheus.yml:/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - prometheus_data:/prometheus</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; ports:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \"9090:9090\"</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; command:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \'--config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml\'<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \'--storage.tsdb.path=/prometheus\'<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \'--web.enable-lifecycle\'</p>\n<p>&nbsp; grafana:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; image: grafana/grafana:latest<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; container_name: grafana<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; restart: unless-stopped</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; ports:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \"3000:3000\"</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; volumes:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - grafana_data:/var/lib/grafana</p>\n<p>&nbsp; loki:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; image: grafana/loki:latest<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; container_name: loki<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; restart: unless-stopped</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; ports:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \"3100:3100\"</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; command: -config.file=/etc/loki/local-config.yaml</p>\n<p>&nbsp; alertmanager:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; image: prom/alertmanager<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; container_name: alertmanager<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; restart: unless-stopped</p>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; ports:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \"9093:9093\"</p>\n<p>volumes:<br>&nbsp; prometheus_data:<br>&nbsp; grafana_data:</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Save and close the file</p>\n<h4>Prometheus Configuration</h4>\n<p>vim /opt/monitoring/prometheus/prometheus.yml</p>\n<p>global:<br>&nbsp; scrape_interval: 15s</p>\n<p>scrape_configs:</p>\n<p>&nbsp; - job_name: \'pgsql-age\'<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; static_configs:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - targets:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \'192.168.0.120:9187\'</p>\n<p>&nbsp; - job_name: \'pgsql-age-node\'<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; static_configs:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - targets:<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - \'192.168.0.20:9100\'</p>\n<p>Save and close the file</p>\n<h4>Start Monitoring Stack</h4>\n<p><span class=\"ͼl\">cd</span> /opt/monitoring<br><br>docker compose up <span class=\"ͼn\">-d</span></p>\n<h4><span class=\"ͼn\">Verify the docker process list</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"ͼn\">docker ps</span></p>\n<h1 data-section-id=\"dlrhay\" data-start=\"3223\" data-end=\"3236\">Access URLs</h1>\n<div class=\"TyagGW_tableContainer\">\n<div class=\"group TyagGW_tableWrapper flex flex-col-reverse w-fit\" tabindex=\"-1\">\n<table class=\"w-fit min-w-(--thread-content-width)\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border-style: solid;\" border=\"1\" data-start=\"3238\" data-end=\"3434\">\n<thead data-start=\"3238\" data-end=\"3255\">\n<tr data-start=\"3238\" data-end=\"3255\">\n<th class=\"\" data-start=\"3238\" data-end=\"3248\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Service</th>\n<th class=\"\" data-start=\"3248\" data-end=\"3255\" data-col-size=\"sm\">URL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody data-start=\"3266\" data-end=\"3434\">\n<tr data-start=\"3266\" data-end=\"3309\">\n<td data-start=\"3266\" data-end=\"3279\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Prometheus</td>\n<td data-start=\"3279\" data-end=\"3309\" data-col-size=\"sm\"><code data-start=\"3281\" data-end=\"3307\">http://192.168.0.19:9090</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-start=\"3310\" data-end=\"3350\">\n<td data-start=\"3310\" data-end=\"3320\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Grafana</td>\n<td data-start=\"3320\" data-end=\"3350\" data-col-size=\"sm\"><code data-start=\"3322\" data-end=\"3348\">http://192.168.0.19:3000</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-start=\"3351\" data-end=\"3388\">\n<td data-start=\"3351\" data-end=\"3358\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Loki</td>\n<td data-start=\"3358\" data-end=\"3388\" data-col-size=\"sm\"><code data-start=\"3360\" data-end=\"3386\">http://192.168.0.19:3100</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-start=\"3389\" data-end=\"3434\">\n<td data-start=\"3389\" data-end=\"3404\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Alertmanager</td>\n<td data-start=\"3404\" data-end=\"3434\" data-col-size=\"sm\"><code data-start=\"3406\" data-end=\"3432\">http://192.168.0.19:9093</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span class=\"ͼn\">Part 3 ... coming soon</span></p>','2026-05-11 10:37:06','Monitoring',NULL,0,0),
(156,'LVM Add Additional Storage on existing partition','add-additional-storage-lvm','<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; text-align: center; line-height: normal;\" align=\"center\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">LVM Add Additional Storage on existing partition</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Here\'s a step-by-step guide to resize your LVM setup to use the newly available space:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 13.5pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Steps to Resize LVM and Filesystem</span></strong></p>\n<ol start=\"1\" type=\"1\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Verify Disk Changes:</span></strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Ensure the disk space has been correctly detected by the system.</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo fdisk -l /dev/sda</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">You should see the new size of </span><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">/dev/sda</span><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">.</span></p>\n<ol start=\"2\" type=\"1\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Resize the Physical Volume:</span></strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Since </span><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">/dev/sda3</span><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\"> is the partition that contains your LVM physical volume, you\'ll need to resize it to use the extra space. The </span><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">parted</span><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\"> tool is suitable for resizing partitions. <strong>Ensure you have backups before performing these operations</strong>.</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">a. Start </span><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">parted</span><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo parted /dev/sda</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">b. Resize the partition:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\">&nbsp;</span>(parted) resizepart 3</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">End?<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\">&nbsp; </span>[48.0GB]? 100GB</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">c. Exit </span><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">parted</span><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\"><span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\">&nbsp;</span>(parted) quit</span></p>\n<ol start=\"3\" type=\"1\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Resize the Physical Volume:</span></strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">After resizing the partition, resize the physical volume to use the new space:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo pvresize /dev/sda3</span></p>\n<ol start=\"4\" type=\"1\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Resize the Logical Volume:</span></strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">To use all available space in the volume group:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/ubuntu-vg/ubuntu-lv</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">If you want to specify a size, you can use:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo lvextend -L +25G /dev/ubuntu-vg/ubuntu-lv</span></p>\n<ol start=\"5\" type=\"1\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Resize the Filesystem:</span></strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Resize the filesystem to match the new size of the logical volume:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo resize2fs /dev/ubuntu-vg/ubuntu-lv</span></p>\n<ol start=\"6\" type=\"1\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Verify Changes:</span></strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Check the filesystem size:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">df -h</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; margin-left: 36.0pt; line-height: normal;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Check the logical volume size:</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo lvdisplay</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 13.5pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Summary LVM add new storage</span></strong></p>\n<ol start=\"1\" type=\"1\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\"><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: \'Times New Roman\',serif; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">Resize Partition:</span></strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">sudo parted /dev/sda</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">(parted) resizepart 3</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0pt 732.8pt; margin: 0cm 0cm 0cm 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10.0pt; font-family: \'Courier New\'; mso-fareast-font-family: \'Times New Roman\'; mso-fareast-language: EN-IN;\">End?<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\">&nbsp; </span>[48.0GB]? 100GB</span></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height: normal; tab-stops: 45.8pt 91.6pt 137.4pt 183.2pt 229.0pt 274.8pt 320.6pt 366.4pt 412.2pt 458.0pt 503.8pt 549.6pt 595.4pt 641.2pt 687.0p